        "original_id": "31d053c4-cb90-590c-8014-c644c04ef8dc",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Nicholas Ray, Elia Kazan, Jerry Wald, Norman Krasna, David Dortort, Horace McCoy, Alfred Hayes, Andrew Solt, Claude Stanush, Stavros\n- Films: Rebel Without a Cause, The Lusty Men, America America, The Anatolian Smile\n- Roles: director, producer, writer, actor, screenwriter\n- Topics: film adaptation, novels, Broadway, Hollywood\n\nFacts:\n- Elia Kazan was born as Elias Kazantzoglou on September 7, 1909.\n- Nicholas Ray was born as Raymond Nicholas Kienzle Jr. on August 7, 1911.\n- America America was adapted from Elia Kazan’s own book, which was published in 1962.\n- America America is a 1963 American dramatic film directed, produced, and written by Elia Kazan.\n- The British title of America America is The Anatolian Smile, which refers to the character Stavros' captivating smile.\n- The Lusty Men was directed by Nicholas Ray.\n- The Lusty Men was produced by Jerry Wald and Norman Krasna.\n- The Lusty Men was based on a novel by Claude Stanush.\n- The screenplay of The Lusty Men was written by David Dortort, Horace McCoy, Alfred Hayes, Andrew Solt, and Jerry Wald.\n- Nicholas Ray was an American film director best known for Rebel Without a Cause.\n- Elia Kazan was a Greek-American director, producer, writer, and actor.\n- The New York Times described Elia Kazan as one of the most honored and influential directors in Broadway and Hollywood history.\n- Nicholas Ray died on June 16, 1979.\n- Elia Kazan died on September 28, 2003.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "15fd9540-d528-5348-9141-4b93e0129072",
